inder dry forests and high temperatures  have sustained this fire since mid-july  sky's mark stone spoke to one of those  working to contain it people saying that  the forests are not being managed in the  way perhaps they used to be the native  americans used to manage them and that  is helping combined with climate change  that is helping to create this disaster  well the drought conditions have  definitely been a challenge to manage  through the pace and scale of  reforestation activities fuel  breaks and fuel and vegetation  treatments has increased across the  forest service here on the plumas  specifically on the dixie fire all of  the land management agencies are  struggling because of the climate change  and the the longer burning season and  the longer fire seasons  well fires have been burning in greece  for nine days now prompting the prime  minister to apologize for a lack of  preparedness  foreign firefighters have now started  work and migrating stalks are being  badly affected by the smoke well sky's  sally lockwood is in arcadia in greece  so sally the government there has  acknowledged that more action uh could  have been taken ahead of these fires how  much is being done to adapt and to  mitigate yeah certainly uh the greek  prime minister addressing the nation  this week did grasp the nettle uh and  talked about at the issue of climate  change which of course is affecting so  much  of the country in terms of these  wildfires which have been burning for  more than a week now he challenged  anyone who didn't believe in climate  change to come and have a look at what's  happening in greece and acknowledge that  the steps taken so far simply do not go  far enough so what is greece doing here  well to put it into context uh greece is  the 11th highest  emitter of carbon uh in the eu germany  the highest uh and it did of course sign  up to the paris uh climate change  agreement it's pledged to reduce its  carbon emissions by more than half by  2030 and 