 weekend where temperatures of 47 degrees  are expected and spain's current  all-time record is 47.3 degrees from  just four years ago  and you may remember the heat dome that  brought extreme temperatures to the  pacific northwest of the united states  and parts of canada or similar  conditions unlikely to return over the  next few days now we might not get the  same highs but temperatures will be well  above august averages possibly by as  much as 10 degrees celsius  so how extreme are those temperatures  and just how much are they being driven  by climate change well i can ask that to  professor peter stott who's a science  fellow in climate attribution at the met  office so welcome to you so can we say  at this stage that these very high  temperatures are the result of climate  change  well we can say that climate change is  playing an important role in these very  high temperatures  it's the weather that brings the  particular meteorological conditions but  it's climate change that's effectively  adding fuel to the fire of that weather  and we looked actually at the canadian  uh extreme temperatures that we saw back  in june when canadian temperatures  reached as high as 49.6 degrees celsius  which is a a massive 4.6 degrees celsius  warmer than the previous record  and found that climate change had made  the chances of having such extreme  temperatures very very much more likely  about 4 000 times more likely be  calculated  so it's very clear that climate change  is making these extreme conditions even  more extreme and giving us these  extraordinary record-breaking  temperatures  well yes you use the word extraordinary  i was going to use that word for the  series of events that we've seen from  heat waves to floods over the last few  weeks and months what have you made of  it i've made of it that  what we climate scientists have been  predicting for  several decades now  is really with us  we've we've very accurately predicted  the rise in global average te