d on by those  extreme temperatures in greece  threatened some of the most famous sites  in all of human history olympia and  marathon  but closer to home we know that climate  change is also putting some of the uk's  most cherished archaeological sites in  danger  experts have told the daily climate show  that more than 10 000 heritage sites  here could disappear  while air pollution can erode the  limestone and sandstone blocks that make  up many ancient structures like the  pyramids of giza in egypt and the rose  granite used in lots of egyptian  artifacts is starting to crack as  fluctuating temperatures cause the rock  to expand and then to contract  and these temperature changes are  damaging cave paintings in indonesia too  as temperatures rise and fall salt  crystals in the cave walls expand and  contract causing the paint to flake and  the iconic maui statues placed along the  edges of rapa nui otherwise known as  easter island are critically at risk and  that's because of coastal erosion  future digs are also in danger  scientists estimate there are at least  180 000 archaeological sites in the  arctic and very few of them have been  excavated but melting ice sheets and  tundra fire means many of these sites  will be lost  well let's focus then on the impact in  the uk now and speak to dr allison  sheridan formerly principal curator of  early prehistory at national museum  scotland and archaeologist of the year  2020 so welcome to you  so tell us what could the impact be on  our archaeological sites and historic  areas of interest in the uk  the greatest threat is from coastal  erosion  where  increasing storminess is going to scour  the coastline we can see it already and  you also have the problem of rising sea  level at the same time so we find that  there are iconic sites such as scarabray  which are now threatened because the sea  is much much closer than it ever was uh  when the site was was first constructed  about five thousand years ago  so what c